 ## Overview
-Cloud Density Sampling generates the base volumetric density of clouds by combining low-frequency 3D noise textures using a weighted sum (Fractal Brownian Motion, FBM) and remapping the result to control cloud shape and coverage [S1, unofficial]. It differs from its siblings by explicitly separating the generation of the base cloud structure (via low-frequency textures and height gradients) from high-frequency detail (via curl noise displacement and high-frequency textures) and by using a specific channel-weighted FBM from a single RGBA texture sample rather than procedural noise functions [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Procedural Volume Cloud Modeling](2256-procedural-volume-cloud-modeling.md) by providing the specific evaluation routine for the density field [S1, unofficial].
+Cloud Density Sampling generates the base volumetric density of clouds by combining low-frequency 3D noise textures using a weighted sum (Fractal Brownian Motion, FBM) and remapping the result to control cloud shape and coverage [S1, unofficial]. It differs from [Procedural 3D Worley-Perlin Fractal Noise Density](5267-procedural-3d-worley-perlin-fractal-noise-density.md) by relying on pre-computed multi-channel 3D textures rather than procedural Worley noise generation, and from [Full Cloud Density Function](5875-full-cloud-density-function.md) by handling the core density evaluation logic (including wind skew and basic height gradients) within this specific sampling routine rather than delegating to a higher-level composite function.
 
